                        <msItem>
                            <title>Psalterium</title>
                            <note>Described by Oliver, <title>Monasticon Diocesis
                                Exoniensis</title>, p. 36: 'A splendid psaltery', '301
                                folios'.</note>
                        </msItem>
                    </msContents>
                    <history>
                        <origin><origDate calendar="#Gregorian" notBefore="1380" notAfter="1399">s.
                            xiv ex</origDate></origin>
                        <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_770">Tywardreath,
                            Cornwall, Benedictine alien priory of St Andrew</orgName> <p>This
                            rejected attribution first appeared in the Supplement to Medieval
                            Libraries of Great Britain (1987). The corresponding Medieval Libraries
                            of Great Britain index card cites Oliver, <title>Monasticon Diocesis
                            Exoniensis</title>, p. 36. There, Oliver claims that 'a splendid
                            psaltery [...] formerly belonged to this priory [i.e. Tywardreath]'.
                            Ker, however, disagreed with the attribution, noting on the card: 'From
                            the diocese of Durham; no evidence whatever for Tywardreath'.</p>
                            <p>Compare, more recently, the British Library <ref
                            target="https://searcharchives.bl.uk/catalog/032-002017548">catalogue
                            entry</ref>: 'the otherwise unsupported 18th-19th cent. attribution of
                            medieval ownership to Tywardreath Priory in Cornwall (see the title on
                            the spine and pencil note at f. 2b) suggests that it may have come to
                            this family through the marriage of Charles, 6th Baron Clifford, with
                            Eleanor Mary, daughter of Henry, 8th Baron Arundell of Wardour Castle,
                            see the references to other material from Tywardreath at Wardour in
                            <title>Hist. MSS. Commission, Second Report</title>, 1870, App., pp.
                            33-36.'</p></provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Belonged to <persName role="fmo">Lord
                            Clifford</persName> of Chudleigh at Ugbrooke Park, Devon in Oliver's
                            time.</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Now British Library Additional MS
                            44949. See E. G. Millar in <title>Archaeologia</title> 87 (1937),
                            1.</provenance>
                    </history>
